
At this time there is not much to do here. This thing just dispatches
POSIX threads which do fairly worthless work. They compute a fibonacci
number using a very horrific approach. The horror is the performance
which is worse than most people can imagine. An excellent source of
"worthless work" that can be thrown away while also providing a result
easily verified. 

At the moment one may do : 

esther$ 
esther$ /usr/bin/nice -n +15 ./main 8 44 2>&1 | grep '^DATA'
DATA : thr(000) fib(33)   5317483024 nsec delta       147120 nsec
DATA : thr(001) fib(26)    205581480 nsec delta       104954 nsec
DATA : thr(002) fib(26)    131891707 nsec delta        98713 nsec
DATA : thr(003) fib(22)     17535155 nsec delta      3002020 nsec
DATA : thr(004) fib(40)  93194883912 nsec delta       757856 nsec
DATA : thr(005) fib(39)  68574545691 nsec delta       100185 nsec
DATA : thr(006) fib(37)  31546554237 nsec delta        98274 nsec
DATA : thr(007) fib(41) 113178636102 nsec delta      2750507 nsec
esther$ 

Above we see that we requested 8 threads and that the upper range for
the fibonacci sequence will be 44. Each thread does the task and we
get timing data in nanoseconds. Above we see that fib(41) was computed
in 113178636102 nsec. This is a number we can at least trust into the
millisecond precision.  At least I hope so. This data above is from a
very slow and very small computer with a VIA Eden Esther processor at
the surprising clock rate of 400MHz. It is the slowest thing that I
had running. An Intel i9 can compute the same result in 8.211 secs and
that is while also dealing with 255 other simultaneous threads.

An old Apple PowerMac with quad 2.5GHz cores is somewhat different :

